#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065287} :activity/id #uuid "eb9087bf-30e8-5a71-8438-1b9d7e3953ca" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051029 :topic/id #uuid "7b7f598a-21a7-593a-99a2-ef8e0258c820" :topic/math-academy-id 323} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065287 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065421 :tutorial/content "Let's think about how to simplify the following:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)}\n$$\n\nThe expression above is a fraction, but there are additional fractions in the numerator and denominator.\nSuch expressions are called **fractions of fractions**.\nWe use parentheses to make it easier to read.\n\nThe trick to working with fractions of fractions is to remember that a fraction represents a division. So, a fraction of fractions is a division of two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{3}}{\\color{red}{4}}\\right)} = \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right) \\boldsymbol{\\div} \\left(\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{3}}{\\color{red}{4}}\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, recall that dividing by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{3}}{\\color{red}{4}}\n$$\n\nis the same as multiplying by its reciprocal\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}{4}}{\\color{blue}{3}}\n$$\n\nWe can use this fact to simplify the fraction above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{2}{3}) \\div (\\frac{3}{4}) &=  \\\\\n(\\frac{2}{3}) \\cdot (\\frac{4}{3}) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2 \\cdot 4}{3 \\cdot 3} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{8}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)} = \\dfrac{8}{9}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5da17ec4-93cc-5398-bbfc-d50405cd2fad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8182 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "000dfe16-b4b5-5050-b07d-e01d48c6261b" :step/math-academy-id 79637 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065288}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065288 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064360 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063947 :question/id #uuid "78675372-1e29-5088-8907-52d9e54124e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-13083"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68cd4840-bb77-5065-884a-ce1c8c309cd0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048392}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064926}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Fraction of Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "d17d6b7d-6849-50e4-a34b-78c672570998" :step/math-academy-id 79638 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065289}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065289 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065422 :tutorial/content "How do we simplify\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}\n$$\n\nNotice that, this time, we have a counting number divided by a fraction.\n\nThe trick is to write\n\n$$\n5 = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}{5}}{\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\nand perform the same process as before:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5)/((\\frac{2}{3})) &=  \\\\\n5 \\div (\\frac{2}{3}) &=  \\\\\n(\\frac{5}{1}) \\div (\\frac{2}{3}) &=  \\\\\n(\\frac{5}{1}) \\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{5 \\cdot 3}{1 \\cdot 2} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{15}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "80cee0a8-050d-53ca-8a81-4b6e37f71cdc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8183 :tutorial/title "Dividing a Counting Number by a Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "e39488bb-4bd7-5dfe-b572-7901ceb4d1c7" :step/math-academy-id 79642 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065290}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065290 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064361 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063948 :question/id #uuid "3bad12f4-14b2-5ab6-a1db-9d460b9a26ca" :question/math-academy-id "e-13084"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ca49b4e-a4fa-5a67-9334-12e8656b5d1a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064781}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Counting Number by a Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "450d83cb-4e1d-5817-81f1-d89746298ceb" :step/math-academy-id 79643 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065291}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065291 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065423 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to simplify a fraction of fractions like\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{5}{3}\\right)}{4}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have a fraction divided by a counting number. We have two methods available.\n\nThe long way is to write\n\n$$\n4 = \\dfrac 4 1\n$$\n\nand then calculate in the same way as we did earlier:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((\\frac{5}{3}))/(4) &=  \\\\\n(\\frac{5}{3}) \\div 4 &=  \\\\\n(\\frac{5}{3}) \\div (\\frac{4}{1}) &=  \\\\\n(\\frac{5}{3}) \\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{5 \\cdot 1}{3 \\cdot 4} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA quicker way is to recognize that the result will have the same numerator as the top fraction and that the denominator is just the product of the two denominators:\n\n$$\n((\\frac{5}{3}))/(4) = \\frac{5}{3 \\cdot 4} = \\frac{5}{12}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "03013ab2-d0a1-57fa-817f-9dd124de63f4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8185 :tutorial/title "Dividing a Fraction by a Counting Number"} :step/id #uuid "05b3f520-be5d-5978-b74c-77dab033ac85" :step/math-academy-id 79647 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065292}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065292 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064362 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063949 :question/id #uuid "87974d27-c494-5b1a-84e9-03e39f3e0b1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-13086"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "56936b9b-2b52-58a7-ae3f-7d51492b8247" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064780}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Fraction by a Counting Number"} :step/id #uuid "8697e561-0d74-5f5b-821a-7c762a1d34a2" :step/math-academy-id 79648}] :activity/title "Fractions of Fractions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064415}]}
